import { ExplanationCopy } from "../../explanation"
import { ReaderCopy } from "../../reader-article"

pub fn copy -> ReaderCopy = ReaderCopy {
  title: "Dependency graphs and cycles",
  summary: (explanation ()).summary,
  purpose: [
    "After splitting code into files, two files may end up importing each other. A dependency graph draws an arrow from each module to the modules it imports. A cycle occurs when following those arrows returns to the starting point.",
    "In the first project, shared-a.ssrg needs shared-b.ssrg, and shared-b.ssrg needs shared-a.ssrg. Preparing a needs b, and preparing b needs a. Change the structure so dependencies can be processed first.",
  ],
  exampleTitle: "Two files that each need the other’s value",
  reading: [
    "The package section of seseragi.toml declares the name, version and supported language versions. Under run, entry = \"main\" selects main.ssrg in the default src directory, and target = \"process\" selects a process application.",
    "In shared-a.ssrg, import { b } attempts to bring in the public name b from shared-b.ssrg. pub let a: Int = b + 1 declares a public integer a. Similarly, shared-b.ssrg imports a and attempts to define b as a + 1. Each file needs the other first.",
    "main.ssrg imports a and defines its output in pub effect fn main. An Effect is a value describing work to execute. show turns an integer into text, and println writes text and a newline. In this project the import cycle is detected before the entry point’s output can run.",
  ],
  rules: [
    "Cycles are forbidden whether an import brings in values, types or traits. A trait defines operations shared by several types; importing only a trait still creates a dependency. There is no exception for type-only cycles.",
    "This includes both two files importing each other directly and longer chains of three or more files that return to their starting point. A re-export using pub import belongs to the same dependency graph as an ordinary import.",
    "An as alias changes the local name, not the target module. Paths that resolve to the same file in the same package identify one module. Changing a spelling or alias alone does not change the direction of a dependency.",
    "Delaying Effect execution leaves the source imports in place. Functions within one module can call each other in a rec group, but rec does not allow separate modules to import each other.",
    "The compiler includes every module path forming the cycle in its diagnostic. Follow the path back to the starting point instead of inspecting only the final import shown.",
  ],
  mistakes: [
    "In the type-cycle project below, user.ssrg and order.ssrg import only types. pub struct User declares a type with a String field named name; pub struct Order declares one with an Int field named count. main constructs a value with User { name: \"Aki\" } and reads the string with .name. The type-only cycle prevents this output from starting too.",
    "Neither type definition in this small example actually uses its imported type. Removing both unnecessary imports removes those cyclic references. If both files really need a shared type, move it into a third file and import it from both. That third file must not import its consumers back.",
  ],
  related: [
    "Check how dependencies initialize before their consumers once cycles are removed.",
    "Distinguish changing a local name from changing the actual import target.",
    "Check why a file that forwards public names also participates in dependencies.",
  ],
}

pub fn title -> String = (copy ()).title

pub fn repairTitle -> String =
  "Move the shared value into a file that depends on neither consumer"

pub fn repair -> Array<String> = [
  "The repair is a separate project. common.ssrg publishes the shared integer with pub let base: Int = 20. shared-a.ssrg stores base + 1 in a, and shared-b.ssrg stores base + 2 in b. Both need common.ssrg, which imports neither of them.",
  "main.ssrg imports a and b. The backtick-delimited string `${a}, ${b}` inserts the two integers into text. Executing the selected main’s Effect lets println write 21, 22 and a newline.",
  "The original cycle has no values that satisfy both a = b + 1 and b = a + 1. This repair changes the design to derive two results from a shared base; it does not preserve those impossible equations. Decide which value the application should share, then change both the actual imports and the calculation accordingly.",
]

pub fn explanation -> ExplanationCopy = ExplanationCopy {
  question: "How do I reorganize two files that import each other?",
  summary: "An import chain that returns to its starting module is a compile error, even for types alone. Moving shared definitions to another module can make dependencies one-way.",
  reading: "This first example is a complete project intended to fail. shared-a.ssrg imports b, while shared-b.ssrg imports a, and each tries to calculate its own value from the other. Importing a in main.ssrg brings that cycle into the project.",
  result: "seseragi run . rejects the project with SES-K0001 and a Cycle diagnostic naming shared-a and shared-b. There is no output. The complete repair below moves a shared base into common.ssrg and prints 21, 22 from the entry point.",
}
